## Figure style for every lecture book — the one place figure colours live.
##
## Every chapter that Quarto executes loads this file (see sitecustomize.py
## beside it), so a chapter's code names colours by their slot — "C0" to "C4"
## — and never by value. Changing a colour here changes it in every figure of
## every book, the next time the chapters are executed.
##
## Hex values are written without "#": matplotlibrc reads "#" as a comment.

## Five colours for data, in order. Not the page palette: two lines on a plot
## have to be told apart by every reader, and the page's indigo, teal and
## amber share one lightness, which leaves red-green colour-blind readers
## with three greys. These keep the page's indigo and vermilion and spread the
## other three in lightness. Any two are at least 15.7 apart in OKLab (x100)
## for normal vision and 9.5 under protanopia, deuteranopia or tritanopia.
##
##   C0  藍  indigo     the default series; what a single plot is drawn in
##   C1  朱  vermilion  the series that matters: a fitted line, an optimum
##   C2  青竹 green
##   C3  山吹 yellow    light; use it for fills and thick marks, not thin lines
##   C4  藤  purple
##
## Neutrals are written as matplotlib greys ("black", "gray", "0.85"). The
## page lays every figure on its paper by multiplying, so a neutral grey
## takes on the paper's warmth by itself.
axes.prop_cycle: cycler('color', ['165e83', 'bc3817', '2e8a6b', 'd9a020', '9a6fb0'])

## Text in the page's ink; frame and ticks a step lighter so the data leads.
text.color:       2a2927
axes.labelcolor:  2a2927
axes.edgecolor:   4a4640
xtick.color:      4a4640
ytick.color:      4a4640
xtick.labelcolor: 2a2927
ytick.labelcolor: 2a2927

## A warm grey as dark as matplotlib's own b0b0b0, so a chapter's
## grid(alpha=0.3) still shows.
grid.color:       a8a196
